<p id="fig53desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;53.</span> Figure&nbsp;53 is a bar graph, where four age groups (12 or older, 12 to 17, 18 to 25, and 26 or older) are shown on the horizontal axis, and the percentage receiving substance use treatment in the past year is shown on the vertical axis.<br>
Percentages of people in 2023 who received substance use treatment in the past year among those who needed substance use treatment in the past year were 23.6&nbsp;percent for people aged 12 or older, 38.9&nbsp;percent for people aged 12 to 17, 16.6&nbsp;percent for people aged 18 to 25, and 24.2&nbsp;percent for people aged 26 or older.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig53">Return to Figure&nbsp;53</a>.</p>

<p id="fig54desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;54.</span> Figure&nbsp;54 is a pie chart, with a label below the chart that says, â€œ1.8 Million Adolescents with a Substance Use Disorder Who Did Not Receive Substance Use Treatment.â€ Among adolescents with a substance use disorder in the past year who did not receive substance use treatment in the past year, the pie chart shows the number and percentage of adolescents who (1)&nbsp;sought treatment, (2)&nbsp;did not seek treatment but thought they should get treatment, and (3)&nbsp;did not perceive a need for substance use treatment.<br>
Among the 1.8&nbsp;million adolescents aged 12 or older in 2023 with a substance use disorder in the past year who did not receive substance use treatment in the past year, 9,000 (0.5&nbsp;percent) sought treatment, 49,000 (2.8&nbsp;percent) did not seek treatment but thought they should get treatment, and 1.6&nbsp;million (96.6&nbsp;percent) did not perceive a need for substance use treatment.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig54">Return to Figure&nbsp;54</a>.</p>

<p id="fig55desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;55.</span> Figure&nbsp;55 is a pie chart, with a label below the chart that says, â€œ39.6 Million Adults with a Substance Use Disorder Who Did Not Receive Substance Use Treatment.â€ Among adults with a substance use disorder in the past year who did not receive substance use treatment in the past year, the pie chart shows the number and percentage of adults who (1)&nbsp;sought treatment, (2)&nbsp;did not seek treatment but thought they should get treatment, and (3)&nbsp;did not perceive a need for substance use treatment.<br>
Among the 39.6&nbsp;million adults aged 18 or older in 2023 with a substance use disorder in the past year who did not receive substance use treatment in the past year, 189,000 (0.5&nbsp;percent) sought treatment, 1.9&nbsp;million (4.8&nbsp;percent) did not seek treatment but thought they should get treatment, and 36.7&nbsp;million (94.7&nbsp;percent) did not perceive a need for substance use treatment.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig55">Return to Figure&nbsp;55</a>.</p>

<p id="fig56desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;56.</span> Figure&nbsp;56 is a horizontal bar graph, where six type and location categories for mental health treatment are shown on the vertical axis, and the percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 who received mental health treatment at each type and location is shown on the horizontal axis. Percentages of adolescents who received mental health treatment for each type and location are shown in descending order.<br>
The percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who received mental health treatment in the past year was 31.9&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who received outpatient mental health treatment in the past year was 23.6&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who received mental health treatment via telehealth in the past year was 14.2&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who took prescription medication to help with their mental health in the past year was 13.9&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who received inpatient mental health treatment in the past year was 3.5&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who received mental health treatment in a prison, jail, or juvenile detention center in the past year was 1.2&nbsp;percent.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig56">Return to Figure&nbsp;56</a>.</p>

<p id="fig57desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;57.</span> Figure&nbsp;57 is a horizontal bar graph, where six type and location categories for mental health treatment are shown on the vertical axis, and the percentage of adolescents aged 12 to 17 with a past year MDE who received mental health treatment at each type and location is shown on the horizontal axis. Percentages of adolescents who received mental health treatment for each type and location are shown in descending order.<br>
Among adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who had a past year MDE, 59.8&nbsp;percent received mental health treatment in the past year.<br>
Among adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who had a past year MDE, 49.1&nbsp;percent received outpatient mental health treatment in the past year.<br>
Among adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who had a past year MDE, 33.6&nbsp;percent received mental health treatment via telehealth in the past year.<br>
Among adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who had a past year MDE, 32.2&nbsp;percent took prescription medication to help with their mental health in the past year.<br>
Among adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who had a past year MDE, 8.1&nbsp;percent received inpatient mental health treatment in the past year.<br>
Among adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 who had a past year MDE, 1.9&nbsp;percent received mental health treatment in a prison, jail, or juvenile detention center in the past year.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig57">Return to Figure&nbsp;57</a>.</p>

<p id="fig58desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;58.</span> Figure&nbsp;58 is a horizontal bar graph, where six type and location categories for mental health treatment are shown on the vertical axis, and the percentage of adults aged 18 or older who received mental health treatment at each type and location is shown on the horizontal axis. Percentages of adults who received mental health treatment for each type and location are shown in descending order.<br>
The percentage of adults aged 18 or older in 2023 who received mental health treatment in the past year was 23.0&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adults aged 18 or older in 2023 who took prescription medication to help with their mental health in the past year was 16.3&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adults aged 18 or older in 2023 who received outpatient mental health treatment in the past year was 14.1&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adults aged 18 or older in 2023 who received mental health treatment via telehealth in the past year was 12.1&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adults aged 18 or older in 2023 who received inpatient mental health treatment in the past year was 1.2&nbsp;percent.<br>
The percentage of adults aged 18 or older in 2023 who received mental health treatment in a prison, jail, or juvenile detention center in the past year was 1.0&nbsp;percent.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig58">Return to Figure&nbsp;58</a>.</p>

<p id="fig59desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;59.</span> Figure&nbsp;59 is a bar graph, where two levels of mental illness in the past year (any mental illness [with or without serious mental illness] and serious mental illness) are shown on the horizontal axis. The vertical axis shows the percentage receiving mental health treatment in the past year. For each level of mental illness, the figure shows four bars, one per each age group (18 or older, 18 to 25, 26 to 49, and 50 or older).<br>
Percentages of adults in 2023 who received mental health treatment in the past year among those who had any mental illness (with or without serious mental illness) were 53.9&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 or older, 51.0&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 to 25, 55.6&nbsp;percent for adults aged 26 to 49, and 52.7&nbsp;percent for adults aged 50 or older.<br>
Percentages of adults in 2023 who received mental health treatment in the past year among those who had serious mental illness were 71.1&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 or older, 65.0&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 to 25, 75.2&nbsp;percent for adults aged 26 to 49, and 66.9&nbsp;percent for adults aged 50 or older.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig59">Return to Figure&nbsp;59</a>.</p>

<p id="fig60desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;60.</span> Figure&nbsp;60 is a horizontal bar graph. The vertical axis shows a category for all adults aged 18 or older and seven racial or ethnic group categories for adults aged 18 or older. Racial or ethnic group categories are shown on the vertical axis in descending order of the associated percentages. The horizontal axis shows the percentage of adults aged 18 or older with any mental illness in that category who received mental health treatment in the past year. Each percentage includes a bar with the associated 99&nbsp;percent confidence interval.<br>
The percentage of adults with any mental illness in 2023 who received mental health treatment in the past year was 53.9&nbsp;percent (with a 99&nbsp;percent confidence interval of 51.9 to 55.8&nbsp;percent).<br>
The percentage of Not Hispanic White adults with any mental illness who received mental health treatment in the past year was 58.7&nbsp;percent (with a 99&nbsp;percent confidence interval of 56.4 to 61.0&nbsp;percent).<br>
The percentage of Not Hispanic Multiracial adults with any mental illness who received mental health treatment in the past year was 56.3&nbsp;percent (with a 99&nbsp;percent confidence interval of 45.2 to 66.8&nbsp;percent).<br>
The percentage of Hispanic adults with any mental illness who received mental health treatment in the past year was 47.4&nbsp;percent (with a 99&nbsp;percent confidence interval of 42.5 to 52.4&nbsp;percent).<br>
The percentage of Not Hispanic Black adults with any mental illness who received mental health treatment in the past year was 43.8&nbsp;percent (with a 99&nbsp;percent confidence interval of 37.4 to 50.5&nbsp;percent).<br>
The percentage of Not Hispanic Asian adults with any mental illness who received mental health treatment in the past year was 34.7&nbsp;percent (with a 99&nbsp;percent confidence interval of 25.9 to 44.6&nbsp;percent).<br>
The percentage of Not Hispanic American Indian or Alaska Native adults with any mental illness who received mental health treatment in the past year is not shown due to low precision.<br>
The percentage of Not Hispanic Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander adults with any mental illness who received mental health treatment in the past year is not shown due to low precision.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig60">Return to Figure&nbsp;60</a>.</p>

<p id="fig61desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;61.</span> Figure&nbsp;61 is a bar graph, where two levels of mental illness in the past year (any mental illness [with or without serious mental illness] and serious mental illness) are shown on the horizontal axis. The vertical axis shows the percentage with a perceived unmet need for mental health treatment in the past year. For each level of mental illness, the figure shows four bars, one per each age group (18 or older, 18 to 25, 26 to 49, and 50 or older).<br>
Percentages of adults in 2023 who perceived an unmet need for mental health treatment in the past year among those with any mental illness (with or without serious mental illness) in the past year were 23.8&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 or older, 36.7&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 to 25, 25.4&nbsp;percent for adults aged 26 to 49, and 11.5&nbsp;percent for adults aged 50 or older.<br>
Percentages of adults in 2023 who perceived an unmet need for mental health treatment in the past year among those with serious mental illness in the past year were 45.2&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 or older, 58.9&nbsp;percent for adults aged 18 to 25, and 44.4&nbsp;percent for adults aged 26 to 49. The percentage for adults aged 50 or older is not shown due to low precision.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig61">Return to Figure&nbsp;61</a>.</p>

<p id="fig62desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;62.</span> Figure&nbsp;62 is a pie chart, with a label below the chart that says, â€œ856,000 Adolescents with a Substance Use Disorder and MDE.â€ The pie chart is split into two pieces. The piece on the left represents adolescents aged 12 to 17 with a substance use disorder and an MDE who did not receive substance use treatment or mental health treatment. The piece on the right represents adolescents aged 12 to 17 with a substance use disorder and an MDE who received substance use treatment or mental health treatment. Additionally, the piece on the right has three slices representing adolescents aged 12 to 17 with a substance use disorder and an MDE who received (1)&nbsp;substance use treatment but not mental health treatment, (2)&nbsp;both substance use treatment and mental health treatment, and (3)&nbsp;mental health treatment but not substance use treatment.<br>
Among the 856,000 adolescents aged 12 to 17 in 2023 with a substance use disorder and an MDE in the past year, 248,000 (28.9&nbsp;percent) received no treatment in the past year and 609,000 (71.1&nbsp;percent) received substance use treatment or mental health treatment in the past year. In addition, 10,000 adolescents with a substance use disorder and an MDE in the past year (1.2&nbsp;percent) received substance use treatment but not mental health treatment, 180,000 (21.0&nbsp;percent) received both substance use treatment and mental health treatment, and 418,000 (48.9&nbsp;percent) received mental health treatment but not substance use treatment.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig62">Return to Figure&nbsp;62</a>.</p>

<p id="fig63desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;63.</span> Figure&nbsp;63 is a pie chart, with a label below the chart that says, â€œ20.4 Million Adults with a Substance Use Disorder and Any Mental Illness.â€ The pie chart is split into two pieces. The piece on the left represents adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and any mental illness who did not receive substance use treatment or mental health treatment. The piece on the right represents adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and any mental illness who received substance use treatment or mental health treatment. Additionally, the piece on the right has three slices representing adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and any mental illness who received (1)&nbsp;substance use treatment but not mental health treatment, (2)&nbsp;both substance use treatment and mental health treatment, and (3)&nbsp;mental health treatment but not substance use treatment.<br>
Among the 20.4&nbsp;million adults aged 18 or older in 2023 with a substance use disorder and any mental illness in the past year, 7.7&nbsp;million (37.6&nbsp;percent) received no treatment in the past year and 12.8&nbsp;million (62.4&nbsp;percent) received substance use treatment or mental health treatment in the past year. In addition, 743,000 adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and any mental illness in the past year (3.6&nbsp;percent) received substance use treatment but not mental health treatment, 3.8&nbsp;million (18.6&nbsp;percent) received both substance use treatment and mental health treatment, and 8.2&nbsp;million (40.2&nbsp;percent) received mental health treatment but not substance use treatment.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig63">Return to Figure&nbsp;63</a>.</p>

<p id="fig64desc" class="endnotes2"><span class="bold nowrap">Figure&nbsp;64.</span> Figure&nbsp;64 is a pie chart, with a label below the chart that says, â€œ6.8 Million Adults with a Substance Use Disorder and Serious Mental Illness.â€ The pie chart is split into two pieces. The piece on the left represents adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and serious mental illness who did not receive substance use treatment or mental health treatment. The piece on the right represents adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and serious mental illness who received substance use treatment or mental health treatment. Additionally, the piece on the right has three slices representing adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and serious mental illness who received (1)&nbsp;substance use treatment but not mental health treatment, (2)&nbsp;both substance use treatment and mental health treatment, and (3)&nbsp;mental health treatment but not substance use treatment.<br>
Among the 6.8&nbsp;million adults aged 18 or older in 2023 with a substance use disorder and serious mental illness in the past year, 1.7&nbsp;million (25.0&nbsp;percent) received no treatment in the past year and 5.1&nbsp;million (75.0&nbsp;percent) received substance use treatment or mental health treatment in the past year. In addition, 160,000 adults aged 18 or older with a substance use disorder and serious mental illness in the past year (2.4&nbsp;percent) received substance use treatment but not mental health treatment, 1.7&nbsp;million (25.4&nbsp;percent) received both substance use treatment and mental health treatment, and 3.2&nbsp;million (47.3&nbsp;percent) received mental health treatment but not substance use treatment.<br>
Long description end. <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Report/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.htm#fig64">Return to Figure&nbsp;64</a>.</p>

